  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain moves in tonight, heavy at times into\nSunday. \n\nSome dry air at low levels, most of the radar returns this\nmorning so far have been at mid levels, but added slight \nchance POPS far western PA to match PBZ, as some rain \nreaching the ground across the far west as of mid morning.\n\nEarlier discussion below.\n\nThe daylight hours today will remain dry despite plenty of \nclouds around. Rainfall will move in from west to east after\ndark, with the steadiest rain after midnight. PWAT values are\nprogged to surge into the 1.60-2.00 inch range by Sunday. A \nsurface low and preceding 45 kt LLJ are progged to track across\nnorthern PA, providing the forcing for the precip. A few to \nseveral hundred J/kg of MUCAPE should support some heavier \ndownpours, but little CAPE above the -10C level suggests the \nlightning threat will be limited to a few rumbles. Instead, \nexpect a blossoming area of stratiform rainfall during the \npredawn hours. Warm cloud depth near 14000 ft aligned with the \nLLJ suggest efficient rainfall with amounts totaling close to \n1.50\" in the northeast quadrant of Pennsylvania. Lesser amounts \nbetween 0.25 and 0.50\" expected across the southern tier. The \nsouthern tier could see a few isolated thunderstorms along a \ntrailing boundary in the later afternoon and evening after the \nearlier batch of stratiform rainfall pushes east of our region. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Rainy pattern continues into much of next week,\nalthough uncertainty remains in the details. On the cool side \nas well.\n\nPoPs are progged to drop below 40 pct on Monday (near 0 pct in\nthe northern tier) as high pressure wedges in from the north. \nHowever, the jet stream will remain active and amplified with \nPacific disturbances moving across the nation. By Tuesday, most \nguidance has a slow moving trough in the Ohio Valley advecting \nwarm air above a wedge of cool sfc air in place from the \naforementioned high pressure system. This setup may linger for \na few days, with up to several inches of rainfall possible \nthrough the week. Cold air damming will also result in high \ntemps only in the 50s to low 60s Tuesday through at least \nThursday. \n\nOne thing we'll need to monitor closely is the large QPF \ngradient / sharp precip cutoff in this type of setup. The same \nhigh pressure system providing steeply sloped isentropes will \nhave very dry air at low levels not far north of the area of \nmaximum isentropic ascent. Model guidance still disagrees on \nexactly where to place the heaviest rain on Tue-Wed, and the \nECMWF in particular has no rain at all across the northern tier.\nLater in the week, a coastal storm will likely develop south \nand east of Long Island, focusing the heaviest rainfall to our \neast (maybe clipping our eastern zones) and wrapping a wedge of \ndry air around the west side of the low.",
